          %p
            %br/
          %h2#the_measurement_problem The “measurement problem”
          %p
            In the context of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretation of quantum mechanics
            it is common to speak of the “measurement problem” when referring to the tension between regarding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability+theory"} probabilistic
            theory and the idea of realism.
          %p
            Namely – by the
            %a{:href => "#Definition"} above
            – a quantum measurement is formally reflected in a change of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probabilities"} probabilities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurement"} measurement
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            one definite outcome is observed, one may wonder how that particular outcome was actually chosen, given that the theory only gives its probability.
          %p (…)
